1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,890
For my project on invertebrates, I chose to study tardigrades.

2
00:00:04,890 --> 00:00:09,780
These are microscopic - or to be more precise - near-microscopic animals.

3
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:14,020
There are well over a thousand known species of these tiny animals

4
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:17,360
which belong to the phylum Tardigrada.

5
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:22,924
Most tardigrades range in length from 0.05 to 1 millimetre,

6
00:00:22,924 --> 00:00:29,500
though the largest species can grow to be 1.2 millimetres in length.

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,239
They are also sometimes called 'water bears':

8
00:00:32,239 --> 00:00:35,400
'water' because that's where they thrive best

9
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,500
and 'bear' because of the way they move.

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:41,980
'Moss piglet' is another name for tardigrades

11
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:45,850
because of the way they look when viewed from the front.

12
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:55,440
They were first discovered in Germany in 1773 by Johann Goeze, who coined the name Tardigrada.

13
00:00:55,440 --> 00:01:00,152
As I say, there are many different species of tardigrade -

14
00:01:00,152 --> 00:01:01,780
too many to describe here

15
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:07,220
but, generally speaking, the different species share similar physical traits.

16
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:13,140
They have a body which is short, and also rounded - a bit like a barrel

17
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:16,690
and the body comprises four segments.

18
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:19,000
Each segment has a pair of legs

19
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:23,920
at the end of which are between four and eight sharp claws.

20
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:28,771
I should also say that some species don't have any claws;

21
00:01:28,771 --> 00:01:33,622
what they have are discs, and these work by means of suction.

22
00:01:33,622 --> 00:01:39,281
They enable the tardigrade to cling on to surfaces or to grip its prey.

23
00:01:39,281 --> 00:01:44,940
Within the body, there are no lungs, or any organs for breathing at all.

24
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:53,340
Instead, oxygen and also blood are transported in a fluid that fills the cavity of the body.

25
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:56,800
As far as the tardigrade's head is concerned,

26
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:01,798
the best way I can describe this is that it looks rather strange -

27
00:02:01,798 --> 00:02:08,333
a bit squashed even, though many of the websites I looked at described its appearance as cute,

28
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:10,640
which isn't exactly very scientific.

29
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:19,450
The tardigrade's mouth is a kind of tube that can open outwards to reveal teeth-like structures known as 'stylets'.

30
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:24,190
These are sharp enough to pierce plant or animal cells.

31
00:02:24,190 --> 00:02:27,040
So, where are tardigrades found?

32
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:32,814
Well, they live in every part of the world, in a variety of habitats:

33
00:02:32,814 --> 00:02:35,220
most commonly, on the bed of a lake

34
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:40,650
or on many kinds of plants or in very wet environments.

35
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:42,780
There's been some interesting research

36
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:48,744
which has found that tardigrades are capable of surviving radiation

37
00:02:48,744 --> 00:02:57,850
and very high pressure, and they're also able to withstand temperatures as cold as -200 degrees centigrade,

38
00:02:57,850 --> 00:03:03,920
or highs of more than 148 degrees centigrade, which is incredibly hot.

39
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:09,865
It has been said that tardigrades could survive long after human beings have been wiped out,

40
00:03:09,865 --> 00:03:13,580
even in the event of an asteroid hitting the earth.

41
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:19,601
If conditions become too extreme and tardigrades are at risk of drying out,

42
00:03:19,601 --> 00:03:22,380
they enter a state called cryptobiosis.

43
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:27,178
They curl into a ball, called a tun - that's T-U-N -

44
00:03:27,178 --> 00:03:29,100
by retracting their head and legs

45
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:35,030
and their metabolism drops to less than one percent of normal levels.

46
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:38,471
They can remain like this until they are re-introduced to water,

47
00:03:38,471 --> 00:03:42,850
when they will come back to life in a matter of a few hours.

48
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:45,320
While in a state of cryptobiosis

49
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:50,730
tardigrades produce a protein that protects their DNA.

50
00:03:50,730 --> 00:03:58,540
In 2016, scientists revived two tardigrades that had been tuns for more than 30 years.

51
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:01,240
There was a report that, in 1948

52
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:12,690
a 120-year-old tun was revived, but this experiment has never been repeated.

53
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:15,548
There are currently several tests taking place in space,

54
00:04:15,548 --> 00:04:17,360
to determine how long tardigrades might be able to survive there

55
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:21,900
I believe the record so far is 10 days.

56
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:26,220
So, erm, moving on. In terms of their diet

57
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:30,660
tardigrades consume liquids in order to survive.

58
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:34,420
Although they have teeth, they don't use these for chewing.

59
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:39,858
They suck the juices from moss, or extract fluid from seaweed,

60
00:04:39,858 --> 00:04:46,780
but some species prey on other tardigrades, from other species or within their own.

61
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:49,215
I suppose this isn't surprising,

62
00:04:49,215 --> 00:04:52,540
given that tardigrades are mainly comprised of liquid

63
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:55,710
and are coated with a type of gel.

64
00:04:55,710 --> 00:05:00,720
Finally, I'd like to mention the conservation status of tardigrades.

65
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:07,773
It is estimated that they have been in existence for approximately half a billion years and,

66
00:05:07,773 --> 00:05:11,740
in that time, they have survived five mass extinctions.

67
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:15,959
So, it will probably come as no surprise to you,

68
00:05:15,959 --> 00:05:21,866
that tardigrades have not been evaluated by the International Union for Conservation of Nature

69
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:24,820
and are not on any endangered list.

70
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:29,380
Some researchers have described them as thriving.

71
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:39,540
Does anyone have any questions they'd like to ask?
